Local fix, no upstream commit yet: bound the 4-bit RLE branch of
readpixels() the way upstream's CVE-2025-15279 fix (#5720 + #5723) bounds
the 8-bit branch.

CVE-2025-15279 is described only as "parsing of pixels within BMP files"
and ZDI's proof of concept is not public (see the #5706 thread: the fix
author never had it). The two upstream commits bound the 8-bit RLE branch
only. The 4-bit branch of the same function writes
head->byte_pixels[ii++] twice per count byte with no check against
height*width, so a 4-bit RLE file overflows the same heap buffer;
fontforge/fontforge#5808 (2026-05-08) has an ASAN reproducer on exactly
that line, closed unfixed under the project's security policy.

Verified at 20251009 with a 4x4 RLE4 bitmap whose first run claims 255
pixels: with only the two upstream commits applied, `importOutlines()`
segfaults; with this patch the import fails cleanly ("Bad input file"),
and a well-formed 4x4 RLE4 bitmap still imports. The package test carries
both fixtures.

The bound is `cnt + (cnt&1)` rather than upstream's `cnt`, because this
branch writes two pixels per iteration and so writes one pixel past an
odd count (the trailing `if ( cnt==-1 ) --ii` cannot fire: the loop only
exits once `cnt` is below -1). A plain `ii + cnt` bound would let that
one extra pixel past the end, and `ii + cnt + 1` would reject
well-formed files whose last run exactly fills the buffer.

Each of those three forms is pinned by a fixture in the package test.
The `(cnt&1)` term needs a file that leaves the write index odd before a
run that ends exactly at the buffer, which `rle4-odd-boundary.bmp` does
with a delta to pixel 1 followed by a 15-nibble run: built with the
plain `ii + cnt` bound FontForge accepts that file and writes index 16
of a 16-byte buffer, and with this patch it is rejected. The two
well-formed bitmaps rule out the `+ 1` form.

